WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.350
Then we can just 
say, 'You are a dog now!'

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:05.350
Welcome back to mCoding. 
I'm James Murphy.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:08.875
Today, we're talking about the difference
between the type of an object

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:11.766
and the dunder class attribute 
of that object.

00:00:11.766 --> 00:00:16.422
And also how to change the class 
of an object at runtime after it's been created.

00:00:16.422 --> 00:00:20.301
It's that time again to talk about the best
sponsor - myself!

00:00:20.301 --> 00:00:22.388
That's right, mCoding is not just 
my YouTube channel.

00:00:22.388 --> 00:00:24.540
It's also my 
software consulting company.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:28.061
We do software consulting, 
contracting, training, and interview prep.

00:00:28.061 --> 00:00:31.262
So, if you need any of those things, 
please do reach out to me.

00:00:31.262 --> 00:00:34.458
You can find out 
more by visiting mcoding.io.

00:00:34.458 --> 00:00:35.478
So, let's dive in.

00:00:35.478 --> 00:00:39.271
If you have any exposure to Python, 
you're probably already familiar with 'type'.

00:00:39.271 --> 00:00:43.323
Pass in any object to the built-in type,
and it tells you what type it is.

00:00:43.323 --> 00:00:47.814
Here, we're implementing the dunder copy for 
the Animal class by getting the type of the object.

00:00:47.814 --> 00:00:52.089
And then creating a new object of that
type, passing in the same name.

00:00:52.089 --> 00:00:53.660
When we define equality 
for our object,

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:58.318
it's also common to not want to 
compare against things that aren't of the same type.

00:00:58.318 --> 00:01:03.210
In this case, we just check if the 
type of 'self' is the same as the type of 'other.'

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.274
And when we're defining a __repr__
for our class,

00:01:05.274 --> 00:01:09.932
it's common to have the name of the 
type in the string representation.

00:01:09.932 --> 00:01:11.891
The reason that we even use 
the type at all

00:01:11.891 --> 00:01:16.582
instead of just hardcoding the name of 
the class 'Animal' is because of inheritance.

00:01:16.582 --> 00:01:20.052
Let's say we had a Dog class 
that inherits from Animal.

00:01:20.052 --> 00:01:24.185
Of course, printing out an Animal, 
we get the expected result.

00:01:24.185 --> 00:01:29.780
But if we create a Dog instead, then it still
prints out 'Animal' instead of 'Dog.'

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.520
But if we use the type of 'self,' then it'll
do the right thing in both cases.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.000
For Animal, it'll print out 'Animal,' and
for a Dog, it'll print out 'Dog.'

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.318
So, that's your pretty typical use case.

00:01:38.318 --> 00:01:40.899
As expected, 'type of self' gives you the
type of 'self.'

00:01:40.899 --> 00:01:43.136
What else is there to say about it?

00:01:43.136 --> 00:01:46.978
Well, if you read enough code, you might see other 
people instead of using 'type of self,'

00:01:46.978 --> 00:01:50.394
instead they're saying 'self.__class__'

00:01:50.394 --> 00:01:53.001
Everywhere that you might 
be expecting to see 'type of self.'

00:01:53.001 --> 00:01:54.796
Instead, you see this other thing.

00:01:54.796 --> 00:01:57.030
So, is one of them right and 
one of them wrong?

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.753
Like, what's the difference?

00:01:58.753 --> 00:02:01.977
Hmm, well, how about we look 
at the CPython source code

00:02:01.977 --> 00:02:05.065
and just get a feel for 
how many times each is used?

00:02:05.065 --> 00:02:09.181
Okay, so there are a decent number 
of occurrences of 'type of self,'

00:02:09.181 --> 00:02:13.389
and there are also a decent 
number of occurrences of 'self.__class__.'

00:02:13.389 --> 00:02:17.465
The fact that they're both pretty widely used
is a bit strange, but okay.

00:02:17.465 --> 00:02:20.506
Maybe you need a flame war 
to see which one's better.

00:02:20.506 --> 00:02:25.790
Go ahead and comment #type or 
#class to let me know which one you like better.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:28.797
I mean, obviously, if you've got class, you
should go with...

00:02:28.797 --> 00:02:30.900
Anyway, back to the differences
 between them.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.894
Rest assured that 99.9% 
of the time,

00:02:33.894 --> 00:02:37.454
they do exactly the same thing, and 
you shouldn't worry about it.

00:02:37.454 --> 00:02:39.497
The number one reason that people
 choose one over the other

00:02:39.497 --> 00:02:44.408
is just pure stylistic preference or just
 copying code that already exists.

00:02:44.408 --> 00:02:46.551
People that prefer '.__class__' 
tend to think that

00:02:46.551 --> 00:02:50.817
using multiple pairs of parenthesis like 
this can be slightly less readable,

00:02:50.817 --> 00:02:54.867
and people that prefer 'type' tend to 
compare it to using other built-ins like len()

00:02:54.867 --> 00:02:59.120
where you wouldn't typically call a __len__ 
you would just use the built-in len()

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.050
Realistically, in most cases, 
just pick one and stick with it.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:03.543
It just doesn't matter.

00:03:03.543 --> 00:03:06.901
So, for the 0.1 percent of the time where
it might actually make a difference,

00:03:06.901 --> 00:03:08.810
here are the actual differences.

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:11.643
The primary difference between using
 'type' and using '__class__'

00:03:11.643 --> 00:03:14.700
is that a class can 
lie about its '__class__.'

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.769
The first way a class can lie is 
just by assigning the '__class__' attribute.

00:03:18.769 --> 00:03:22.341
If you were to use __class__, you would
see the lie 'SomeClass,'

00:03:22.341 --> 00:03:26.870
But the built-in 'type' can't be fooled. 
It will always give you the real type of the object.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:29.825
The class could also lie like this: making
__class__ a property.

00:03:29.825 --> 00:03:35.329
Or it could even lie by overriding the 
__getattribute__ and specifically checking for __class__.

00:03:35.329 --> 00:03:40.409
I mean, if you do this, 
you must have incredible job security.

00:03:40.409 --> 00:03:44.389
Now, as obviously wrong and bad as
 doing something like this seems,

00:03:44.389 --> 00:03:47.532
maybe if someone's doing this, 
they have a good reason.

00:03:47.532 --> 00:03:51.778
There's probably some testing mock
 library somewhere that depends on this behavior.

00:03:51.778 --> 00:03:56.269
So, that's an actual functionality reason
to use __class__ over 'type.'

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:59.926
One big thing to watch out for though is
not an object lying about its class,

00:03:59.926 --> 00:04:03.555
but an object changing its 
class at runtime.

00:04:03.555 --> 00:04:05.484
Getting back to the Animal 
and Dog example,

00:04:05.484 --> 00:04:10.307
we can see that we can 
actually assign to the '__class__' attribute.

00:04:10.307 --> 00:04:12.319
So, we can create an Animal.

00:04:12.319 --> 00:04:15.159
If we use 'type' or __class__, 
we see 'Animal.'

00:04:15.159 --> 00:04:18.056
Then we can just say, 
'You are a Dog now!'

00:04:18.056 --> 00:04:22.199
At which point, both 'type' 
and __class__ now tell you 'Dog.'

00:04:27.459 --> 00:04:31.286
That's right. It's not just lying to you 
and telling you that its __class__ is 'Dog,'

00:04:31.286 --> 00:04:35.326
but its type is actually 'Animal.' 
It is actually a Dog now.

00:04:35.326 --> 00:04:39.180
This can obviously go wrong in 
so many ways.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:42.820
For starters, the object was created 
and initialized as an Animal.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:45.983
If Animal defined an __init__ function,
that would have been called here,

00:04:45.983 --> 00:04:49.247
but if Dog had defined an __init__ 
function, in this case, it didn't.

00:04:49.247 --> 00:04:52.421
But if it did, 
it was never called.

00:04:52.421 --> 00:04:56.301
So, the Dog part of this Animal may 
not have been initialized.

00:04:56.301 --> 00:04:57.444
You could also do it 
the other way:

00:04:57.444 --> 00:05:00.749
Start with the Dog and then tell it, 
'You're just an Animal now.'

00:05:00.749 --> 00:05:04.732
Just like before, whether you use 'type' 
or the __class__ attribute, they both agree

00:05:04.732 --> 00:05:07.815
it starts out as a Dog 
and then it becomes an Animal.

00:05:07.815 --> 00:05:12.107
If you try calling a method that only Dog has, 
that Animal doesn't, you won't find it.

00:05:12.107 --> 00:05:14.505
This is also, in general, 
not a safe operation,

00:05:14.505 --> 00:05:17.633
although it kind of looks like upcasting,
 which would be safe.

00:05:17.633 --> 00:05:21.863
In this case, it suffers from kind of 
the reverse problem as in the previous one.

00:05:21.863 --> 00:05:26.188
Here, this is initialized as a Dog, 
and it may contain Dog-specific state.

00:05:26.188 --> 00:05:28.232
but the Animal class doesn't know 
about any of that state,

00:05:28.232 --> 00:05:32.129
and it might end up accidentally misusing it 
because it doesn't know that it's there.

00:05:32.129 --> 00:05:34.986
But you can do even weirder 
because the new type that you're turning it into

00:05:34.986 --> 00:05:38.364
doesn't have to be related 
at all to the old one.

00:05:38.364 --> 00:05:40.001
It doesn't have to be 
a superclass,

00:05:40.001 --> 00:05:43.683
it doesn't have to be a subclass, 
it doesn't have to be anywhere in the MRO.

00:05:43.683 --> 00:05:48.174
So, before, Bork is an Animal, and then he's a Car,

00:05:48.174 --> 00:05:52.088
and this is made possible because, 
in order to convert from one class to another,

00:05:52.088 --> 00:05:58.002
there's pretty much only one restriction, 
and that is that the classes need to have a compatible layout.

00:05:58.002 --> 00:06:01.445
Well, how's it even possible that an Animal
and a Car have the same layout?

00:06:01.445 --> 00:06:04.605
Didn't the Animal have a 'name' attribute,
but the Car doesn't?

00:06:04.605 --> 00:06:07.979
Most Python objects are just a 
wrapper around their instance dictionary,

00:06:07.979 --> 00:06:11.821
which you can see a proxy of 
by accessing the __dict__ attribute.

00:06:11.821 --> 00:06:16.010
This is why it's possible to set and delete
arbitrary attributes at runtime.

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:20.146
This means you shouldn't have any 
trouble converting between any two normal classes.

00:06:20.146 --> 00:06:23.050
It is possible to have classes that don't
have compatible layouts, though.

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:25.793
For instance, if you use __slots__ in one
but not in the other.

00:06:25.793 --> 00:06:27.721
If you're trying to mutate the 
class of an object,

00:06:27.721 --> 00:06:31.110
the 'to' and 'from' need 
to have the exact same slots.

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:33.197
So, if one has slots and 
the other doesn't,

00:06:33.197 --> 00:06:36.618
or if they have different 
slots, then they won't be compatible.

00:06:36.618 --> 00:06:39.209
You'll also have trouble converting 
to or from most built-ins

00:06:39.209 --> 00:06:44.179
or anything that inherits from them as those 
typically have their own custom layouts.

00:06:44.179 --> 00:06:48.292
So, how about an example where you 
might actually want to do this that's not totally crazy?

00:06:48.292 --> 00:06:52.087
I mean, it's still mostly crazy 
but not totally crazy.

00:06:52.087 --> 00:06:56.424
This one's actually straight out of the docs,
so you know it can't be too crazy.

00:06:56.424 --> 00:06:58.427
When you define 
a module in Python,

00:06:58.427 --> 00:07:03.549
you have basically no control over 
the type of the module object that's created.

00:07:03.549 --> 00:07:08.389
You just write your .py file, and then when
you import it, somehow magically, a module is there.

00:07:08.389 --> 00:07:11.451
By default, modules 
have the built-in 'ModuleType`.

00:07:11.451 --> 00:07:14.930
But modules are objects just like everything
else in Python, so you might have wanted to

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:17.669
customize the behavior of your module.

00:07:17.669 --> 00:07:21.210
You can actually do that by defining your
own module type, and then setting the '__class__'

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:25.160
attribute of the current module to be that
module type.

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:29.509
In this case, we're just modifying '__setattr__'
so if someone sets a value on our module like

00:07:29.509 --> 00:07:32.990
a global variable, then it'll 
print out a message.

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:36.949
But in theory, you could do whatever logging
or other behavior that you want.

00:07:36.949 --> 00:07:42.525
The other use case I have is a way of deserializing
in-memory representations of objects.

00:07:42.525 --> 00:07:48.150
Suppose I've got some data stored in a class,
and that class has some non-trivial internal state.

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:53.298
Then, suppose that I want to restore or
 deserialize an object that had a given state.

00:07:53.298 --> 00:07:56.478
Say, 'x' was 1 and 
the internal state was 4.

00:07:56.478 --> 00:07:59.285
Depending on the structure 
and implementation of this class,

00:07:59.285 --> 00:08:03.539
it may be difficult to construct 
an instance that has this given state.

00:08:03.539 --> 00:08:06.369
You may not know the sequence 
of events that led it to be in this state,

00:08:06.369 --> 00:08:09.324
or you may not want to repeat 
that sequence even if you know it.

00:08:09.324 --> 00:08:14.161
Instead, you might like to just have that
object sort of pop into existence with the given state.

00:08:14.161 --> 00:08:18.000
So, what we'll do is create a Blank class
that has a compatible layout.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:19.662
Then, we can just reach 
into the object

00:08:19.662 --> 00:08:23.940
and update its instance dictionary to 
match the exact state that we're looking for.

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:27.643
Finally, modify the '__class__' 
to be our desired class.

00:08:27.643 --> 00:08:32.229
At this point, once again, 'type' and '__class__'
will agree that the class is 'SomeData.'

00:08:32.229 --> 00:08:37.595
What might have been cons or points to 
watch out for before can now be used to our benefit.

00:08:37.595 --> 00:08:41.410
Creating an object this way doesn't 
go through any of the normal mechanisms.

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:45.828
It doesn't get initialized with its '__init__,'
it wasn't created using the '__new__,.

00:08:45.828 --> 00:08:49.558
And it didn't have its metaclass's 
'__call__' called either.

00:08:49.558 --> 00:08:52.206
Now, normally, you would 
want all those things to happen.

00:08:52.206 --> 00:08:56.500
But in this case where you just want the
 object to pop into existence at a known given state,

00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:59.299
maybe you don't want those 
things to happen.

00:08:59.299 --> 00:09:04.188
If you're familiar with the built-in 'pickle,' this is 
similar but even more extreme than what 'pickle' does.

00:09:04.188 --> 00:09:07.017
'Pickle' will create the object 
using the '__new__' of the class.

00:09:07.017 --> 00:09:12.192
And then it'll update the dictionary, or if you 
have a '__setstate__' defined, it'll use that instead.

00:09:12.192 --> 00:09:15.573
But a class's '__getstate__' and '__setstate__'
may not be something that you control,

00:09:15.573 --> 00:09:17.779
especially if you didn't 
write the class.

00:09:17.779 --> 00:09:22.130
Additionally, that representation may 
not be just a copy of the instance dictionary.

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:27.071
It could be compressed or just different variables
stored or some things just recomputed once it's unpickled.

00:09:27.071 --> 00:09:29.294
I mean, don't get me wrong, 
if you can use 'pickle,'

00:09:29.294 --> 00:09:31.929
then you should definitely 
use 'pickle' over doing this.

00:09:31.929 --> 00:09:34.620
But if you need to, 
this is one use case.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:35.730
Once again, I'm James Murphy.

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:37.209
Thank you for watching.

00:09:37.209 --> 00:09:39.414
